+# yaml.v2 migration survey: stopped to preserve behavior
+
+No callers were migrated. The seven import edges exist, and yaml.v3 is already
+present, but substituting v3 does not preserve the existing buildbuddy.yaml parse
+contract. Following the instruction to stop rather than force a behavior-changing
+change, this commit records the survey only.
+
+## Verified behavior
+
+Compared the repository's exact versions, yaml.v2 v2.4.0 and yaml.v3 v3.0.1,
+using a standalone Go program outside the repository. The probe used structured
+config/action/resource-request fields matching the workflow parser, including
+`*time.Duration` and `any`. Both libraries used non-strict `Unmarshal`, as
+`config.NewConfig` does. Results:
+
+| YAML input | v2 | v3 |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Empty input or `---` | Empty config, no error | Same |
+| Unknown top-level field alongside actions | Ignored, no error | Same |
+| `resource_requests: {cpu: 1, memory: 1.5}` | `int(1)`, `float64(1.5)` | Same |
+| `resource_requests: {disk: {size: 1}}` | `map[interface{}]interface{}` | `map[string]interface{}` |
+| `resource_requests: {cpu: on}` | `bool(true)` | `string("on")` |
+| `resource_requests: {memory: 2026-10-12}` | `string("2026-10-12")` | `time.Time` |
+| `actions: [{name: first, name: last}]` | Accepted; name is `last` | Error: mapping key already defined |
+| `actions: [{timeout: 1000}]` | Accepted; timeout is 1 microsecond | Error: cannot unmarshal `!!int` into `time.Duration` |
+
+These are observable differences. For example, `GetEstimatedCPU` returns an empty
+string for v2's boolean `on`, but would return `"on"` for v3's string; the date
+case changes `GetEstimatedMemory` in the opposite direction. Duplicate keys and
+numeric durations turn previously accepted workflows into errors. Existing
+workflow tests cover basic configs, multiline commands and invalid YAML, but do
+not cover these compatibility cases.
+
+Preservation may be possible with a separately implemented and validated v2
+compatibility layer; this survey does not claim it is mathematically impossible.
+An import/BUILD substitution alone is demonstrably insufficient. In particular,
+v3's duplicate-key check is enabled internally without a public decoder option to
+disable it. Simply choosing non-strict decoding does not restore v2 behavior.
+I did not introduce a new compatibility decoder while reporting this as a
+dependency simplification, or silently narrow the accepted input contract.
+
+## Before / after
+
+Ran the same commands before and after the survey:
+
+```sh
+rg -l 'gopkg.in/yaml.v2' --glob '*.go' | wc -l
+rg -l '@in_gopkg_yaml_v2//:yaml_v2' --glob BUILD | wc -l
+```
+
+| Metric | Before | After |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| First-party Go source files importing yaml.v2 | 8 | 8 |
+| BUILD files declaring the yaml.v2 external label | 8 | 8 |
+| In-scope importing source files / BUILD files | 7 / 7 | 7 / 7 |
+| Removed import edges / targets | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |
+| Production source LOC changed | 0 | 0 |
+
+The eighth importer is `cli/translate/yaml`, which was left untouched. No external
+module became unused. The grep evidence above still shows eight first-party
+yaml.v2 users. No binary-size or build-time savings are claimed.
+
+## Verification commands
+
+The standalone comparison ran successfully (exit 0):
+
+```sh
+mkdir -p /tmp/yaml-migration-survey
+cd /tmp/yaml-migration-survey
+go mod init yaml-survey
+go get gopkg.in/yaml.v2@v2.4.0 gopkg.in/yaml.v3@v3.0.1
+# main.go contains the structured comparison described below.
+go run .
+```
+
+For reproduction, use these types with both libraries' `Unmarshal`, and compare
+the errors and values for each input in the table (wrap resource-request examples
+in `actions: [{resource_requests: ...}]`):
+
+```go
+type Config struct {
+    Actions []Action `yaml:"actions"`
+}
+type Action struct {
+    Name string `yaml:"name"`
+    Timeout *time.Duration `yaml:"timeout"`
+    ResourceRequests ResourceRequests `yaml:"resource_requests"`
+}
+type ResourceRequests struct {
+    CPU any `yaml:"cpu"`
+    Memory any `yaml:"memory"`
+    Disk any `yaml:"disk"`
+}
+```
+
+Probe source and output are at `/tmp/yaml-migration-survey/main.go` and
+`/tmp/yaml-migration-survey/results.txt` on this VM. This was a differential
+decoder experiment, not a repository build or test-suite pass.
+
+No Bazel builds or tests were needed because no source or BUILD target changed.
+The direct-rdeps survey query was launched in the background with `nohup`, logging
+to `/tmp/yaml-rdeps.log`:
+
+```sh
+bazel query 'rdeps(//..., set(//cli/config:config //cli/plugin:plugin //enterprise/server/cmd/ci_runner:ci_runner_lib //enterprise/server/hostedrunner:hostedrunner //enterprise/server/workflow/config:config //enterprise/server/workflow/service:service //enterprise/server/test/integration/ci_runner:ci_runner_test), 1)' --output=label
+```
+
+The query was cancelled with SIGINT during package loading after deciding to stop
+the migration; there are no changed targets needing a reverse-dependency test
+set. It did not produce a completed target list, and no query success is claimed.
+
+## Scope and omissions
+
+Only this explicitly requested report was added outside the package-source scope.
+No shared dependency files, lockfiles, source, tests or BUILD files were edited.
+No golden tests were added because the migration was stopped at the premise
+verification stage. No changes were pushed and no PR was opened.
